        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Israel and Hezbollah continue to trade blows despite the ceasefire Middle East Issued on: 08/05/2026 - 11:41 Modified: 08/05/2026 - 11:46 Israel has taken responsibility for Wednesday night's strike on the southern suburbs of Beirut, with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu saying he personally authorised the attack to kill a top Hezbollah Commander identified as Malek Balou. The continued ceasefire incursions are further pressuring talks between Tel Aviv and Beirut, which are being supported by the US. Hezbollah initially indicated it would support a deal if it was respected by Israel but the two parties continue to trade blows, despite the truce. Details by Eliza Herbert. Iran and the US trade fire over Hormuz, Trump says ceasefire still in effect 08/05/2026 Middle East Iran on the verge of economic asphyxiation -- rare footage from Tehran 08/05/2026 Middle East China's 'key priorities': Open Hormuz, get oil flowing, avoid 'getting dragged into conflicts' 06/05/2026 Asia / Pacific 'Significant threat to local ecosystem: Israel's war on Lebanon generated 16 million tons of rubble' 06/05/2026 Middle East Iran Nobel winner Narges Mohammadi at risk of dying in custody 06/05/2026 Middle East US suspends Hormuz operation: Washington believes close to deal with Iran 06/05/2026 Middle East Iran Nobel Peace Prize winner Narges Mohammadi 'between life and death' 06/05/2026 Middle East US-Iran conflict reaches painful stalemate: US will respond if fired on 06/05/2026 Middle East US pauses Hormuz escorts, Donald Trump says progress on Iran deal 06/05/2026 Middle East"
